.SHORT TERM...
Issued at 228 PM PST Mon Nov 2 2026
(This evening through Tuesday night)

Widespread cloud cover is building in ahead of tonight`s rain
showers. Showers approach the coast late tonight as a surface low
pressure system moves southwards offshore of Northern California.
This is a cold-core system and will bring in a much colder airmass,
resulting in well below normal temperatures at the surface. Highs
will be in the upper 50s to mid 60s with the coolest locations
across the higher elevations. So when will you start feeling
impacts? The latest high resolution guidance shows the main rain
band approaching the coast around ~5-6AM with showers reaching the
coastal North Bay by 7-8AM and filtering into the rest of the Bay
Area through the remainder of the morning. There is a low (< 10%)
chance of thunderstorms with this first rain band moving onshore.
Rain totals are forecast to be highest across the coastal North Bay
Mountains from Sonoma to Marin with between 0.5-0.75". The rest of
the North Bay and the Santa Cruz Mountains will see 0.25-0.5" while
the remainder of the Bay Area sees less than a tenth of an inch. The
heaviest rain will impact the morning commute so remember to slow
down, allow extra time to reach your destination, and be prepared
for slick roads.

The more interesting possibility, thunderstorm wise, is a secondary
band of discrete showers that develops offshore after the initial
rainband moves inland. These showers are characterized by a more
discrete (individual) look and develop in a more unstable marine
environment. Thunderstorm chances increase to 15-20% for the coastal
North Bay and northern outer waters late Tuesday morning through
late Tuesday afternoon as the second round develops and moves
inland. The second round is interesting for a few reasons. The first
is that the more discrete nature of the storms may allow for further
strengthening and will increase the likelihood of thunderstorm
development. Cooler air aloft will filter in behind the surface low
and promote the formation ice crystals, should a storm develop,
increasing the potential for lightning. Forecast sounding data
suggests good low level shear in the lowest 0 to 1 kilometers with
decent speed and directional shear. The stronger the shear the more
likely the storm is to rotate which increases the potential for
waterspouts to develop. Current thinking is that waterspouts will be
possible offshore with this second round of storms. The caveat is
that we will likely need some sort of instability generation (i.e.
clouds clearing and warming the surface over the ocean) to instigate
offshore thunderstorm development. If we do not see that clearing
then the potential for thunderstorms drops off significantly. Either
way, it will definitely be worth keeping an eye on the forecast and
