.LONG TERM...
Issued at 228 PM PST Mon Nov 2 2026
(Wednesday through next Sunday)

Conditions warm up and dry out Wednesday through the weekend as the
surface low dissipates/the upper level trough progresses eastward
and ridging builds. This will see a return of more seasonal to
slightly above normal temperatures in the upper 60s to 70s with
patchy 80s across far interior locations. Overnight lows remain more
mild in the 40s to 50s. Diurnally breezy winds return along the
coast and across mountain gaps/passes but no major concerns with
regards to this. Rain chances increase sometime around the 10/11
with the CPC and ensembles highlighting a likely prolonged period of
rain across the West Coast mid-month.&&

.AVIATION...
(18Z TAFS)
Issued at 1023 AM PST Mon Nov 2 2026

VFR conditions prevail as high clouds blanket the
majority of the region this morning. These conditions will continue
through the rest of today, with a pattern change occuring by Tuesday
morning. An upper level low will cut across northern California
brining rain to the Bay Area and perhaps into the northern area
Central Coast region. The best chances look to be over North Bay and
around SF Bay, with chances decreasing as you go inland. Started
bringing -RA into the North Bay around 15Z, though some models show
a 20% chance of it starting around 12Z for KSTS and areas north and
west of there. Rain chances continue to spread to the southeast
through the morning with showers being possible for the afternoon.
We continue to keep an eye out at the thunderstorm chance.
Probabilities over Sonoma County range from 0-20% favoring the west
side of the region. Opted to not add any thunder to KSTS for the
time being, but if thunder were to happen the best time frame would
be from 18Z-22Z based on the latest guidance. Expect MVFR cigs with
rain, though lower cig and vis are possible with stronger bands or
cells of rain.

Vicinity of SFO...High clouds, VFR conditions, and drainage winds
